steps.body:
To keep the k largest numbers from a stream, use a *min* heap and never let it hold more than k. Push each number, and when the size passes k, pop the top: the smallest of the k + 1, which can't be one of the k largest. At the end the heap holds the k largest, and its top is the kth largest. Each push and pop costs O(log k), so n numbers take O(n log k):

```cpp
std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> top;
for (int x : {3, 2, 1, 5, 6, 4}) {
    top.push(x);
    if (top.size() > 2) {
        top.pop();
    }
}
std::cout << top.top() << '\n';   // 5, the 2nd largest
```

The same works for any order. For the k most frequent words, count them in a map, then keep `(count, word)` pairs in a heap whose top is the *weakest* pair kept: the lowest count, and on a tie the later word. A lambda can be the comparator too. Each lambda has its own unnamed type, and `decltype(strongerLater)` names it in the heap's template arguments. In C++17 the heap can't make a lambda of that type on its own, so pass the lambda to the constructor as well:

```cpp harness
using Entry = std::pair<int, std::string>;
{{code}}
pq.push({2, "love"});
pq.push({3, "i"});
pq.push({2, "code"});
std::cout << pq.top().second << '\n';
```

```cpp
auto strongerLater = [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {
    if (a.first != b.first) {
        return a.first > b.first;   // a higher count comes out later
    }
    return a.second < b.second;     // a tie: the earlier word comes out later
};
std::priority_queue<Entry, std::vector<Entry>, decltype(strongerLater)> pq(strongerLater);
```

When the stream ends, pop everything into a vector. It comes out weakest first, so `std::reverse` it to list the strongest first.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

using Entry = std::pair<int, std::string>;

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    int k = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> k;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> counts;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::string word;
        std::cin >> word;
        ++counts[word];
    }

    auto strongerLater = [](const Entry& a, const Entry& b) {
        if (a.first != b.first) {
            return a.first > b.first;
        }
        return a.second < b.second;
    };
    std::priority_queue<Entry, std::vector<Entry>, decltype(strongerLater)> heap(strongerLater);
    for (const auto& [word, count] : counts) {
        heap.push({count, word});
        if (static_cast<int>(heap.size()) > k) {
            heap.pop();
        }
    }

    std::vector<Entry> best;
    while (!heap.empty()) {
        best.push_back(heap.top());
        heap.pop();
    }
    std::reverse(best.begin(), best.end());
    for (const auto& [count, word] : best) {
        std::cout << word << ' ' << count << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

