steps.body:
To report the median while numbers keep arriving, split them into two halves. `low` is a max heap holding the smaller half, so its top is the biggest of them. `high` is a min heap holding the larger half, so its top is the smallest of them. Keep `low` the same size as `high` or one bigger, and the median sits on the tops:

```cpp harness
std::priority_queue<int> low;
std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> high;
for (int x : {5, 15, 1}) {
    {{code}}
}
std::cout << low.top() << ' ' << high.top() << '\n';
```

```cpp
low.push(x);
high.push(low.top());       // the biggest of the low half moves up
low.pop();
if (high.size() > low.size()) {
    low.push(high.top());   // low keeps the extra one
    high.pop();
}
```

Passing every number through `low` first keeps the halves in order: everything in `low` stays at most everything in `high`. With an odd count the median is `low.top()`. With an even count it is the average of the two tops, `(static_cast<double>(low.top()) + high.top()) / 2`, converted to `double` before the sum so it can't overflow and the division keeps its half. Each number costs a few heap operations, O(log n), instead of sorting everything again.

example.code:
#include <functional>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::priority_queue<int> low;
    std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> high;
    std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x = 0;
        std::cin >> x;
        low.push(x);
        high.push(low.top());
        low.pop();
        if (high.size() > low.size()) {
            low.push(high.top());
            high.pop();
        }
        double median = 0;
        if (low.size() > high.size()) {
            median = low.top();
        } else {
            median = (static_cast<double>(low.top()) + high.top()) / 2;
        }
        std::cout << median << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

