steps.body:
For each day, how many days until a warmer one? Checking every later day is O(n^2). Instead, keep a stack of the days still waiting for a warmer one. Their temperatures only go down from the bottom of the stack to the top, which is why it's called a *monotonic stack*. When a new day is warmer than the day on top, that day's wait is over: record it and pop, and keep popping while the new day beats the top. Then push the new day:

```cpp
std::vector<int> temps = {73, 74, 75, 71, 69, 72, 76, 73};
std::vector<int> wait(temps.size(), 0);
std::stack<int> waiting;   // indexes, not temperatures
for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(temps.size()); ++i) {
    while (!waiting.empty() && temps[i] > temps[waiting.top()]) {
        wait[waiting.top()] = i - waiting.top();
        waiting.pop();
    }
    waiting.push(i);
}
for (int w : wait) {
    std::cout << w << ' ';   // 1 1 4 2 1 1 0 0
}
```

Every index is pushed once and popped at most once, so the loop is O(n) even with the inner `while`. Days still on the stack at the end never found a warmer day and keep their 0. Store indexes rather than values: an index gives you the distance, and `temps[i]` gives you the value.

The same idea at both ends of a `std::deque` finds the maximum of every window of k items: pop smaller values off the back before pushing, and pop the front once its index falls out of the window, so the front is always the window's maximum:

```cpp
std::vector<int> nums = {1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7};
int k = 3;
std::deque<int> window;   // indexes, values going down from front to back
for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
    while (!window.empty() && nums[window.back()] <= nums[i]) {
        window.pop_back();
    }
    window.push_back(i);
    if (window.front() <= i - k) {
        window.pop_front();
    }
    if (i >= k - 1) {
        std::cout << nums[window.front()] << ' ';   // 3 3 5 5 6 7
    }
}
```

The deque suits a window that only slides forward. For the maximum of any range, asked in any order while the values change, *Range Queries: Fenwick and Segment Trees*, later in this section, builds a segment tree.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <stack>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> temps(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cin >> temps[i];
    }

    std::vector<int> wait(n, 0);
    std::stack<int> waiting;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        while (!waiting.empty() && temps[i] > temps[waiting.top()]) {
            wait[waiting.top()] = i - waiting.top();
            waiting.pop();
        }
        waiting.push(i);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if (i > 0) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << wait[i];
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

